//
// Probe: the eight local-bus words (D87, RFC-0007 §C8 criteria 1 and 2).
//
// The reference's bus is process-global and in-process — crossbeam channels in
// a `BTreeMap<String, (Sender, Receiver)>` (bus/mod.rs `PIPES`), not zenoh. So
// a round trip is reproducible in one process with no network, which is what
// makes this probe hermetic at all.
//
// Each case is one claim the first draft of RFC-0007 got wrong or could not
// have known:
//
//   - `send` pushes a bool; `send.quick` pushes nothing. That is the whole
//     difference between them.
//   - An existing-but-empty channel answers NODATA. `"in"` and `"out"` exist
//     before any program runs, so they are the only way to reach that arm
//     without creating a channel first. An *absent* channel is an error, and
//     **no probe can carry that half**: a word's error renders through the
//     reference's own diagnostic table, which F66 already records as text no
//     second machine reproduces, so a probe for it would be a third approved
//     deviation for the frame rather than the message. The exact text is
//     asserted in `crates/bund2-stdlib/src/bus.rs` instead.
//   - `bus.data` creates the channel it reports on, so it answers false and
//     then a `recv` on the same name is NODATA rather than an error.
//   - `bus.data.current` takes the channel name from the current stack, so a
//     stack and a channel share one namespace.
//   - `send.` takes its *object* from the workbench; `recv.` takes its *name*
//     from the workbench and pushes the value to the stack. The asymmetry is
//     the reference's.
//
"probe: send answers a bool, and the value comes back" println
"p1" 42 send
"p1" recv

"probe: in exists before the program does, so it is empty and not absent" println
"in" recv

"probe: bus.data creates the channel it reports on" println
"p2" bus.data
"p2" recv

"probe: bus.data.current names the channel after the current stack" println
bus.data.current

"probe: send.quick answers nothing" println
"p3" 7 send.quick
"p3" recv

"probe: send. takes the object from the workbench, the name from the stack" println
"p4" 9 { true } ?. send.
"p4" recv

"probe: recv. takes the name from the workbench and pushes to the stack" println
"p5" 5 send.quick
"p5" { true } ?. recv.

"probe: send.quick. takes the object from the workbench and answers nothing" println
"p6" 3 { true } ?. send.quick.
"p6" recv

"probe: a channel keeps order" println
"p7" 1 send.quick
"p7" 2 send.quick
"p7" recv
"p7" recv
